  [PASS] K07d.5 THE ONE THING THAT WORKS, and it is qualitative: inside the pre-declared trust range the binned median of g_obs - g_bar rises, turns over and DECLINES, which neither of the two competing kernels permits -- they both saturate monotonically. The turnover is within a factor 2 of the predicted location   (observed turnover at g_bar = 2.77 a_0 against the predicted 2.54 a_0; the median falls to 0.700 a_0 by g_bar = 9.1 a_0, below the 0.500 plateau the equation book's kernel would sit at and far below the 1.000 of MOND's simple kernel)
  [PASS] K07d.6 AGAINST INTEREST: the AMPLITUDE at the turnover is 30-40% above the predicted 0.6476 a_0, which by (D2) is what a stellar mass-to-light ratio about 10% away from 0.5 would do. So even the surviving half of the candidate cannot be quoted as a measurement of a_0 -- it is a measurement of Upsilon at the worst possible leverage   (peak 0.9294 a_0 vs predicted 0.6476 (+44%); at leverage -3.92 x 0.85 that is a Upsilon shift of +11.5%)

  the Upsilon_[3.6] that puts the observed ceiling ON the predicted 0.6476 a_0: 0.540 (stellar populations give 0.50 +- 0.10; k07's marginalised fit gives 0.44; item 102's self-consistent value 0.61)
  [PASS] K07d.7 read the other way round -- with a_0 fixed by Lambda, the ceiling MEASURES the stellar mass-to-light ratio, and the value it returns is a stellar-population value. That is the only defensible use of this landmark, and it is a Upsilon meter rather than an a_0 meter   (Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.540 from the ceiling alone, against SPS 0.50 +- 0.10)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.  THE ALTERNATIVE.  A dark halo has no ceiling on g_obs - g_bar at all, so the bound is a genuine
    discriminant in principle -- it is the measurement, not the idea, that fails.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  For an NFW halo Delta = G M_halo(<r)/r^2 is set by the halo's own mass and concentration and can take any
  value; there is no acceleration at which it must peak. The framework forbids Delta > 0.648 a_0 anywhere.
  The bound is therefore falsifiable in the strong sense -- and SPARC does falsify it at the assumed Upsilon.
  [PASS] K07d.8 the per-GALAXY version fails too, so the point-by-point failure is not a handful of bad radii: nearly half of SPARC's galaxies have at least one point above the ceiling   (44% of 147 galaxies exceed the ceiling somewhere (canonical); 35% (alt))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.  MUTATION CONTROLS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] MK07d.1 data built to obey the kernel exactly must obey the ceiling exactly -- otherwise the ceiling is mis-derived rather than falsified   (max Delta on synthetic kernel-obeying curves with SPARC's own g_bar = 1.000000 x the ceiling)
  [PASS] MK07d.2 injecting a_0 = 0.25x canonical must move the ceiling by exactly 0.25x -- the landmark is strictly proportional to a_0 and is therefore an a_0 meter in principle   (max Delta / (h* x 0.25 a_0) = 1.000000000 on a continuum grid)
  [PASS] MK07d.2 injecting a_0 = 4.0x canonical must move the ceiling by exactly 4.0x -- the landmark is strictly proportional to a_0 and is therefore an a_0 meter in principle   (max Delta / (h* x 4.0 a_0) = 1.000000000 on a continuum grid)
  [PASS] MK07d.3 the turnover in section 4 is a property of the PAIRING of g_obs with g_bar, not of their marginal distributions: shuffling which g_obs goes with which g_bar destroys it   (shuffled median Delta/a_0 in the peak bin (1.5 < y < 4.5) = -1.472 against the real 0.929)


